Data Availability and Usage for CBWAE EEG Pipeline
1. Dataset Overview

This study utilizes EEG recordings from 100 subjects performing motor imagery tasks. Each subject has multiple recording sessions (runs) in EDF format, named according to the convention:

S###R##.edf


S### → Subject ID (e.g., S001 for Subject 1).

R## → Run number (e.g., R04 or R06).

For consistency and reproducibility, only R04 and R06 runs were selected for analysis. These runs were chosen because they were complete for all subjects and provide sufficient data for Leave-One-Subject-Out (LOSO) evaluation.

Note: Other runs exist for some subjects, but were excluded to maintain uniformity across all participants.

2. Preprocessing and Features

The main processing pipeline, implemented in cbwae_novel_loso_quantum.py, performs the following steps:

Load EEG data from EDF files.

Segment the signal into 2-second windows.

Extract classical EEG features (mean, variance, skewness, kurtosis, bandpower) and optional wavelet features.

Compute quantum-inspired features to augment the classical feature set.

Train multiple base classifiers (XGBoost, Logistic Regression, Random Forest, kNN, SVM) and a meta-learner DNN using LOSO cross-validation.

This setup allows other researchers to replicate the results or extend the pipeline to new datasets.

3. Accessing Data for Testing

Due to size constraints, full EDF files are not hosted on GitHub. However, researchers can test the pipeline using:

Public EEG datasets:

BCI Competition IV Dataset 2a: http://www.bbci.de/competition/iv/

Download a subset of subjects and runs.

Rename files to match the format S###R##.edf for compatibility with the scripts.

Sample files:

A small set of EDF files with the proper format can be included in the repository for quick testing.

This ensures full reproducibility without requiring access to the complete raw dataset.

4. GitHub Repository

The complete project code, along with sample data and instructions, is available at:
GitHub Repository: CBWAE_EEG

Repository contents:

CBWAE_EEG/
 ├── cbwae_novel_loso_quantum.py    # Main LOSO pipeline
 ├── check_missing_edf.py            # Utility to verify EDF files
 ├── sample_DATA/                    # Small EDF subset for testing
 ├── README.md                       # Detailed instructions
 └── requirements.txt                # Python dependencies


Note: Large EDF files can be hosted via Git LFS or external repositories (e.g., Zenodo or OSF) to bypass GitHub size limits. Links can be provided in the README or paper.

5. Recommended Usage for Researchers

Testing pipeline: Use the included sample EDF files or publicly available datasets.

Full-scale analysis: If raw EDF data is available, place them in the DATA/ folder and set the DATA_ROOT path in cbwae_novel_loso_quantum.py.

Reproducibility: Ensure all dependencies are installed (requirements.txt) and follow the README for running the LOSO pipeline.

Researchers are encouraged to extend the pipeline by including additional runs, adding new feature extraction methods, or testing with alternative classifiers.

